\title{The Fundamental Group as a Complete Invariant\\
and the Exact Failure of Completeness for Coverings}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{17 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We determine precisely how much of a topological situation the fundamental group remembers.
On the positive side, for aspherical one-dimensional spaces --- the Eilenberg--MacLane spaces
$K(G,1)$ --- the fundamental group is a \emph{complete} invariant: two such spaces are
equivalent if and only if their fundamental groups are isomorphic, and every group is realised.
On the negative side, the fundamental group is emphatically \emph{not} a complete invariant of
coverings of a fixed $K(G,1)$, and we identify the invariant that is.

Working in the groupoid model, in which a $K(G,1)$ is the one-object groupoid of $G$ and a
covering of it is the action groupoid of a $G$-set, we establish: (i) a covering is connected
iff its monodromy action is transitive, its fundamental group at a chosen point of the fibre is
the stabiliser of that point, and its number of sheets is the index of that stabiliser; (ii) the
\emph{Galois correspondence} --- two connected coverings are isomorphic iff their subgroups are
conjugate, morphisms correspond to sub-conjugacy, and pointed isomorphism corresponds to
equality of subgroups; (iii) the deck group of the covering classified by $H\le G$ is
$N_G(H)/H$, so a covering is regular iff $H$ is normal, the universal cover is simply connected
with deck group $G$, and the resulting exact sequence identifies group extensions with regular
coverings; (iv) fibre products decompose into components indexed by double cosets
$H\backslash G/K$, the component through a point being classified by $H\cap gKg^{-1}$.

We then quantify the failure. Over the Klein four group $V$ we exhibit two double coverings with
equivalent total spaces and equal degrees that are not isomorphic as coverings, and we
universalise this: for \emph{every} group $G$ surjecting onto $\Z/2$, the space
$K(G\times \Z/2,1)$ carries two non-isomorphic double coverings whose total spaces are both
$K(G,1)$. Contrasting with $S_3$, where two \emph{distinct} point stabilisers give
\emph{isomorphic} coverings, we show that conjugacy is exactly the right equivalence. Finally we
compute: for the circle the number of sheets is a complete invariant; for the torus the number
of connected $n$-sheeted coverings is exactly $\sigma(n)=\sum_{d\mid n}d$, every finite-index
subgroup of $\Z^2$ is isomorphic to $\Z^2$, and hence all of these infinitely many pairwise
non-isomorphic coverings have a torus as total space. We identify connected double coverings of
any $K(G,1)$ with the nonzero classes of $H^1(G;\mathbb{F}_2)$, and give the exact odd-prime
correction.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The fundamental group $\pi_1(X,x_0)$ of a pointed connected space is the set of loops at $x_0$
modulo homotopy, with concatenation as multiplication. The first question one asks of any
invariant is how much it forgets. The answer here has two faces.

\textbf{Face one: total recall.} Restricted to spaces whose only homotopy is in dimension one
--- the aspherical spaces $K(G,1)$, characterised by $\pi_1=G$ and $\pi_n=0$ for $n\ge 2$ ---
the fundamental group is a complete invariant. Homotopy theory in this range is group theory,
exactly.

\textbf{Face two: exact amnesia.} Change the objects from spaces to \emph{coverings} of a fixed
$K(G,1)$. Now the fundamental group fails maximally: there are pairs of coverings of the same
base with the same number of sheets and homotopy-equivalent total spaces that are not isomorphic
as coverings. What is invisible is not the isomorphism type of the classifying subgroup $H\le G$
but its \emph{position} in $G$; the exact invariant is the conjugacy class of $H$.

\subsection{The groupoid model}

A \emph{groupoid} is a category in which every morphism is invertible. The fundamental groupoid
of a space has points as objects and homotopy classes of paths as morphisms. For a connected
space, choosing a path from a basepoint to each other point yields an equivalence with the
one-object groupoid on $\pi_1$. Homotopy $1$-types are groupoids; connected homotopy $1$-types
are one-object groupoids.

\begin{definition}[Model of $K(G,1)$]
For a group $G$, let $\BG$ be the groupoid with one object $\star$ and
$\Hom(\star,\star)=G$, composition being multiplication.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A groupoid $\mathcal{C}$ is \emph{connected at} an object $c$ if every object is isomorphic to $c$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Complete invariance for homotopy $1$-types]\label{thm:complete}
Let $\mathcal{C},\mathcal{D}$ be groupoids connected at $c$ and $d$. If $\Aut(c)\cong\Aut(d)$ as
groups then $\mathcal{C}\simeq\mathcal{D}$. In particular $\mathbf{B}G\simeq\mathbf{B}H$ iff
$G\cong H$, and every group is realised.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Choose isomorphisms $u_x:c\to x$ for all $x$ with $u_c=\mathrm{id}$, and similarly $v_y$ in
$\mathcal{D}$. Transport an arrow $f:x\to x'$ to $u_{x'}^{-1}fu_x\in\Aut(c)$, apply the given
group isomorphism, and transport back. The chosen isomorphisms cancel in pairs, giving
functoriality; essential surjectivity and full faithfulness are immediate. The converse is
functoriality of $\Aut$ under equivalence.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Coverings as $G$-sets}

\begin{definition}[Action groupoid]
Let $G$ act on a set $X$. The action groupoid $G\ltimes X$ has objects $X$ and
$\Hom(x,y)=\{g\in G: g\cdot x=y\}$. The forgetful functor $G\ltimes X\to\BG$ is the
\emph{covering projection} and $X$ is the \emph{fibre}.
\end{definition}

This is the algebraic shadow of monodromy: a covering of $X$ determines an action of $\pi_1(X)$
on the fibre, and is recovered from it.

\begin{definition}
For $G$-sets $X,Y$, a \emph{morphism of coverings} is a $G$-equivariant map $X\to Y$; an
\emph{isomorphism of coverings} is an equivariant bijection.
\end{definition}

\section{Structure of a connected covering}

\begin{theorem}[Connectivity equals transitivity]
$G\ltimes X$ is connected at $x\in X$ iff $G$ acts transitively on $X$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Transitivity supplies an arrow $g:x\to y$ whenever $g\cdot x = y$. Conversely, given arrows
$e:x\to a$ and $f:x\to b$, the element $fe^{-1}$ carries $a$ to $b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The fundamental group of a covering is a stabiliser]\label{thm:stab}
For $x\in X$ the automorphism group of $x$ in $G\ltimes X$ is canonically isomorphic to
$\Stab_G(x)=\{g\in G:g\cdot x=x\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
If $G$ acts transitively on $X$ then $G\ltimes X\simeq \mathbf{B}\Stab_G(x)$: a connected
covering of a $K(G,1)$ is a $K(H,1)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Degree equals index]\label{thm:degree}
If $G$ acts transitively on $X$ then $|X|=[G:\Stab_G(x)]$ for any $x\in X$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The orbit map $g\mapsto g\cdot x$ induces a bijection $G/\Stab_G(x)\to X$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
The map $\Aut_{G\ltimes X}(x)\to G$ induced by the projection is injective with image exactly
$\Stab_G(x)$.
\end{proposition}

Conversely, every subgroup $H\le G$ is realised by $X=G/H$ with left translation.

\section{The Galois correspondence}

The identity $\Stab_G(g\cdot x)=g\Stab_G(x)g^{-1}$ makes conjugacy unavoidable: moving the
chosen point of the fibre conjugates the subgroup.

\begin{lemma}[Transport]\label{lem:transport}
Let $X$ be a transitive $G$-set with $x_0\in X$, and $Y$ any $G$-set with $y_0\in Y$ such that
$\Stab_G(x_0)\le\Stab_G(y_0)$. Then $f(a\cdot x_0)=a\cdot y_0$ is a well-defined morphism of
coverings, and is the unique one sending $x_0$ to $y_0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $a\cdot x_0=b\cdot x_0$ then $b^{-1}a\in\Stab_G(x_0)\le\Stab_G(y_0)$, so
$a\cdot y_0=b\cdot y_0$. Equivariance and uniqueness are immediate since every point of $X$ is
$a\cdot x_0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Morphisms equal sub-conjugacy]
For transitive $G$-sets $X,Y$ with points $x,y$, a morphism $X\to Y$ exists iff
$\Stab_G(x)\le g\Stab_G(y)g^{-1}$ for some $g\in G$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Galois correspondence]\label{thm:galois}
For transitive $G$-sets $X,Y$ with points $x,y$: $X\cong Y$ as coverings iff
$\Stab_G(y)=g\Stab_G(x)g^{-1}$ for some $g\in G$. Hence isomorphism classes of connected
coverings of a $K(G,1)$ correspond bijectively to conjugacy classes of subgroups of $G$, the
covering attached to $H$ being $G/H$, with total space a $K(H,1)$ and degree $[G:H]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
An isomorphism preserves stabilisers, and transitivity supplies $g$ with $g\cdot e(x)=y$.
Conversely, given the conjugacy, apply Lemma~\ref{lem:transport} in both directions between
$g\cdot x$ and $y$; the composites fix every point $a\cdot x$, hence are identities.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pointed classification]
There is an isomorphism $e:X\to Y$ with $e(x)=y$ iff $\Stab_G(x)=\Stab_G(y)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Abelian base]\label{cor:abelian}
If $G$ is abelian, isomorphism classes of connected coverings correspond bijectively to
subgroups of $G$, and every covering is regular.
\end{corollary}

\section{Deck transformations, regularity, extensions}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{deck group} of a covering $X$ is its group of covering automorphisms.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Deck group]\label{thm:deck}
$\Deck(G/H)\cong N_G(H)/H$, and the deck group acts freely: a deck transformation with a fixed
point is the identity.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
An equivariant self-map of $G/H$ is determined by the image $nH$ of the trivial coset, and
equivariance forces $n\in N_G(H)$; $n\mapsto(aH\mapsto anH)$ is surjective with kernel $H$.
Freeness holds because two equivariant maps out of a transitive $G$-set agreeing at one point
agree everywhere.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Regularity]
The deck group acts transitively on the fibre iff $H\trianglelefteq G$; then
$\Deck(G/H)\cong G/H$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Universal cover]
For $H=1$, i.e.\ $X=G$ with left translation, the covering is simply connected and its deck
group is $G$, a deck transformation being right translation.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact sequence of a regular covering]
For $H\trianglelefteq G$ the sequence $1\to H\to G\to\Deck(G/H)\to 1$ is exact.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Extensions are regular coverings]
A surjection $\varphi:G\twoheadrightarrow Q$ with kernel $N$ is precisely the data of a regular
covering of $K(G,1)$ with total space a $K(N,1)$ and deck group $Q$; every regular covering
arises this way.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Nielsen--Schreier via coverings]
If $G$ is free, the fundamental group of every connected covering of $K(G,1)$ is free;
equivalently, every subgroup of a free group is free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Fibre products and double cosets]
For $H,K\le G$ the fibre product of $G/H$ and $G/K$ is $(G/H)\times(G/K)$ with the diagonal
action. Its components correspond to the double cosets $H\backslash G/K$, the component
containing $(H,gK)$ being classified by $\Stab_G(H,gK)=H\cap gKg^{-1}$. The fibre product is
connected iff $G=HK$.
\end{theorem}

\section{The failure of $\pi_1$ as an invariant of coverings}

\subsection{The Klein four group}

Let $V=C_2\times C_2$, $H_1=C_2\times 1$, $H_2=1\times C_2$, and $H_\Delta=\ker(\mu)$ with
$\mu(x,y)=xy$.

\begin{theorem}[Exactly three double coverings]\label{thm:klein3}
$H_1,H_2,H_\Delta$ are precisely the index-two subgroups of $V$, so $K(V,1)$ has exactly three
connected double coverings, pairwise non-isomorphic.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
An index-two subgroup of a group of order four has order two, hence is generated by one of the
three involutions. Non-isomorphism follows from Corollary~\ref{cor:abelian}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\pi_1$ is not complete for coverings]\label{thm:fail}
The coverings $V/H_1$ and $V/H_2$ have equivalent total spaces (both $K(\Z/2,1)$), the same
number of sheets, and are not isomorphic as coverings.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The swap automorphism of $V$ carries $H_1$ onto $H_2$, so $H_1\cong H_2\cong\Z/2$ and
Theorem~\ref{thm:complete} gives the equivalence of total spaces. Both indices are $2$. Finally
$H_1\neq H_2$ and $V$ is abelian, so Corollary~\ref{cor:abelian} forbids an isomorphism.
\end{proof}

\subsection{A universal source of such pairs}

\begin{theorem}[Twisted pair]\label{thm:twisted}
Let $\varphi:G\twoheadrightarrow C_2$ be any surjection and work over $K(G\times C_2,1)$. Put
$N_{\mathrm{base}}=G\times 1$ and $N_{\mathrm{tw}}=\{(g,\varphi(g)):g\in G\}$. Both have index
two, both are isomorphic to $G$, the two associated double coverings have equivalent total
spaces (each a $K(G,1)$), and they are not isomorphic as coverings.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each is the kernel of a surjective character to $C_2$, hence of index two; $\mathrm{pr}_1$
restricts to an isomorphism on each. Index-two subgroups are normal, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:galois} reduces isomorphism to equality, and the two subgroups differ because
$\varphi\neq 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Taking $G=S_3$ with $\varphi$ the sign character gives two non-isomorphic double coverings of
$K(S_3\times C_2,1)$, both with total space a $K(S_3,1)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Index two kills conjugacy]
Over any base, two connected double coverings classified by $H$ and $L$ are isomorphic iff
$H=L$.
\end{lemma}

\subsection{Conjugacy is exactly right}

\begin{theorem}[$S_3$: distinct subgroups, isomorphic coverings]
Let $S_3$ act on $\{0,1,2\}$ with point stabilisers $P_0\neq P_1$. Then $P_1=\tau P_0\tau^{-1}$
for $\tau=(0\,1)$, and the two three-sheeted coverings $S_3/P_0$ and $S_3/P_1$ are isomorphic.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Synthesis]
Abstract isomorphism of subgroups is strictly coarser than isomorphism of coverings (Klein four
group); equality of subgroups is strictly finer ($S_3$); conjugacy is exactly isomorphism of
coverings.
\end{theorem}

\section{Coverings of the circle: degree is complete}

\begin{theorem}
Every subgroup of $\Z$ is $n\Z$, and two subgroups of $\Z$ with the same index are equal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Classification for the circle]
Two connected coverings of a $K(\Z,1)$ are isomorphic iff they have the same number of sheets.
Every $n\ge1$ occurs, via $n\Z\le\Z$; infinite degree occurs only for the trivial subgroup, the
universal cover $\mathbb{R}\to S^1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Z$ is abelian, so isomorphism is equality of subgroups; degree is index; and index determines
the subgroup.
\end{proof}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:klein3} this does not generalise: over $V$ there are three pairwise
non-isomorphic coverings of the same degree $2$.

\section{Coverings of the torus: $\sigma(n)$ of them, all tori}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:rank}
Every finite-index subgroup $H\le\Z^2$ is isomorphic to $\Z^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $[\Z^2:H]=k$ then $k\Z^2\le H$, so $H$ has rank at least two; as a submodule of a free module
of rank two over the principal ideal domain $\Z$ it is free of rank at most two.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:tori}
The total space of every connected finite-sheeted covering of the torus is again a torus.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Three double coverings]
$\Z^2$ has exactly three subgroups of index two, spanned respectively by $(1,0),(0,2)$; by
$(2,0),(0,1)$; and by $(1,1),(1,-1)$, the last being $\{(x,y):x+y \text{ even}\}$. The three
coverings are pairwise non-isomorphic and all total spaces are tori.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Four triple coverings]
$\Z^2$ has exactly four subgroups of index three, pairwise non-isomorphic as coverings, all with
torus total spaces.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Such a subgroup is the kernel of a surjection $\chi:\Z^2\to C_3$, determined by its values
$(a,b)\in(\Z/3)^2$ on the standard generators and surjective iff $(a,b)\neq(0,0)$. Rescaling by
$2$ preserves the kernel, so the eight nonzero pairs fall into four classes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Prime degree]\label{thm:prime}
For a prime $p$ the torus has exactly $p+1$ connected coverings of degree $p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
There are $p^2-1$ surjective characters $\Z^2\to C_p$; taking kernels is onto the index-$p$
subgroups with all fibres of size $p-1$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:mult}); and $(p^2-1)/(p-1)=p+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
For $a,d>0$ and $c\in\Z$, let $L(a,c,d)\le\Z^2$ be the sublattice spanned by $(a,0)$ and
$(c,d)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Index]
$[\Z^2:L(a,c,d)]=ad$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A two-step tower: $L(a,c,d)\le\Z\times d\Z$, the latter of index $d$ as the kernel of reduction
of the second coordinate; inside it, $L(a,c,d)$ is the kernel of the surjection
$(x,y)\mapsto x-(y/d)c \bmod a$, of relative index $a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hermite normal form]
Every finite-index $H\le\Z^2$ equals $L(a,c,d)$ for unique $a,d>0$ and $0\le c<a$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $a$ generate $\{x:(x,0)\in H\}$ and $d$ generate $\{y:\exists x,(x,y)\in H\}$; choose
$(c,d)\in H$ and reduce $c$ modulo $a$. Clearing second coordinates shows these two elements
generate $H$. Uniqueness holds because $a$, $d$ and $c\bmod a$ are intrinsic to $H$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The $\sigma$ classification]\label{thm:sigma}
For $n\ge1$,
\[
\#\{H\le\Z^2 : [\Z^2:H]=n\} \;=\; \sum_{a\mid n} a \;=\; \sigma(n),
\]
these coverings are pairwise non-isomorphic, and each total space is again a torus.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Index-$n$ subgroups biject with pairs $(a,c)$, $a\mid n$, $d=n/a$, $0\le c<a$; summing the $a$
choices of $c$ over divisors gives $\sigma(n)$. Non-isomorphism is
Corollary~\ref{cor:abelian}; the total spaces are tori by Corollary~\ref{cor:tori}.
\end{proof}

Thus $\sigma(2)=3$, $\sigma(3)=4$, $\sigma(4)=7$, $\sigma(p)=p+1$.

\begin{corollary}[Maximal failure of $\pi_1$]
The torus admits infinitely many pairwise non-isomorphic connected coverings --- $\sigma(n)$ in
degree $n$ --- and every total space is a torus.
\end{corollary}

\section{Double coverings are mod-two characters}

\begin{theorem}[Characters versus kernels]\label{thm:char2}
For any group $G$: a homomorphism $\varphi:G\to C_2$ is nontrivial iff surjective, and then
$[G:\ker\varphi]=2$; every index-two subgroup $H$ is the kernel of the composite
$G\to G/H\cong C_2$; and a homomorphism into $C_2$ is determined by its kernel.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Double coverings equal nonzero mod-two classes]\label{thm:h1}
The assignment $\varphi\mapsto\ker\varphi$ is a bijection
\[
\{\varphi\in\Hom(G,C_2):\varphi\neq1\}\;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\;\{H\le G:[G:H]=2\},
\]
so isomorphism classes of connected double coverings of a $K(G,1)$ correspond to the nonzero
classes of $H^1(G;\mathbb{F}_2)=\Hom(G,\Z/2)$, the covering attached to $\varphi$ having total
space a $K(\ker\varphi,1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Prime index and cyclic characters]
For $p$ prime and $H\trianglelefteq G$ of index $p$, $H=\ker\chi$ for a surjection
$\chi:G\twoheadrightarrow C_p$; conversely the kernel of such a surjection has index $p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicity $p-1$]\label{thm:mult}
Two surjections $G\twoheadrightarrow C_p$ have the same kernel iff they differ by an
automorphism of $C_p$; since $|\Aut(C_p)|=p-1$, exactly $p-1$ surjective characters share each
kernel.
\end{theorem}

At $p=2$ this degenerates to $1$, recovering Theorem~\ref{thm:char2}.

\begin{theorem}[Where irregularity can live]\label{thm:minfac}
Let $G$ be finite and $H\le G$ of prime index $p$ with $H$ not normal. Then $p$ is strictly
larger than the smallest prime factor of $|G|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]
For $S_3$ acting on $\{0,1,2\}$, the stabiliser $P_0$ has index three, is not normal, and is
self-normalising. Hence the associated three-sheeted covering of $K(S_3,1)$ is not regular and
its deck group is trivial, while the smallest prime factor of $|S_3|=6$ is $2<3$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{A. Classify coverings of a $K(G,1)$, $G$ finite.} Enumerate subgroups; group them into
conjugacy classes; each class is one isomorphism class of connected covering, of degree
$[G:H]$, with total space $K(H,1)$, deck group $N_G(H)/H$, regular iff $N_G(H)=G$.

\textbf{B. Count $n$-sheeted coverings of the torus.} For each divisor $a\mid n$ set $d=n/a$ and
let $c$ run over $0,\dots,a-1$; output $L(a,c,d)$. There are $\sigma(n)$ outputs.

\textbf{C. Reduce a sublattice to normal form.} Compute $d=\gcd$ of second coordinates and a
Bézout combination $(c,d)$; clear second coordinates and let $a=\gcd$ of the remaining first
coordinates; reduce $c$ modulo $a$; the index is $ad$.

\section{Discussion}

The two headline results are not in tension: a covering is not a space but a space together with
a projection, and the projection remembers the embedding $H\hookrightarrow G$, not the abstract
group $H$. The dictionary is summarised below.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Covering-theoretic notion & Algebraic counterpart\\
\midrule
connected covering & transitive $G$-set $G/H$\\
number of sheets & index $[G:H]$\\
$\pi_1$ of total space & $H$\\
isomorphism of coverings & conjugacy of subgroups\\
pointed isomorphism & equality of subgroups\\
deck group & $N_G(H)/H$\\
regular covering & $H$ normal\\
universal cover & $H=1$\\
covering morphism & sub-conjugacy $H\le gKg^{-1}$\\
fibre product components & double cosets $H\backslash G/K$\\
double covering & nonzero class in $H^1(G;\mathbb{F}_2)$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Index two is special in two independent ways: index-two subgroups are automatically normal
(killing conjugacy) and $\Aut(C_2)$ is trivial (killing the character ambiguity). Only the
conjunction makes the cohomological count exact.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Odd-prime counting]
For an odd prime $p$,
\[
\#\{\text{connected degree-}p\text{ coverings of }K(G,1)\}
=\frac{p^d-1}{p-1}+\#\{\text{conjugacy classes of non-normal index-}p\text{ subgroups}\},
\]
where $d=\dim_{\mathbb{F}_p}\Hom(G,C_p)$.
\end{conjecture}

The first term counts the regular coverings: nonzero characters modulo scaling by
$(\Z/p)^\times$. For $p=2$ both corrections degenerate, which is why the cohomological count is
then exact; for odd $p$ both switch on and are independent. Theorems~\ref{thm:mult} and
\ref{thm:minfac} supply the ingredients.

Further directions: extend Theorem~\ref{thm:sigma} to $\Z^r$, where the count is governed by
$\zeta(s)\zeta(s-1)\cdots\zeta(s-r+1)$ and all finite coverings of the $r$-torus should again be
$r$-tori; compare with surface groups of genus $g\ge2$, where the total space genus
$n(g-1)+1$ does vary with the degree, isolating the role of abelianness; characterise the finite
groups admitting a non-normal subgroup of prime index $p$ and count the resulting non-regular
coverings, whose deck groups interpolate between the trivial group and $C_p$; and ask for the
two-dimensional shadow, namely the classification of gerbes by $H^2$.

\section{Conclusion}

For aspherical one-dimensional spaces the fundamental group is a complete invariant. For
coverings of such a space it is not, and the failure can be made as bad as one likes: over the
torus, infinitely many pairwise non-isomorphic coverings all have a torus as total space. The
precise invariant is the conjugacy class of the classifying subgroup, and every classical
construction of covering theory is the shadow of an equally classical construction of group
theory.

\end{document}

